==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|huzdan|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|kusdʰo-||something hidden, hiding-place|lang=ine-pro}}, from {{recons|kew-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|kewə-||to cover, encase|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with Old Saxon ''hord'' ‘treasure, hiding-place’, Old High German [[hort]] (German [[Hort]] ‘hoard, protection, refuge’), Old Norse [[hodd]] (Swedish [[hudda]] ‘shelter’), Gothic {{term|𐌷𐌿𐌶𐌳|sc=Goth|tr=huzd||hidden hoard}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/hoːrd/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|g=n|pl=hord}}

# a [[hoard]], especially of valuable items, hidden for preservation

